<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josip Jelačić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Josip Jelačić was ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> from 1848 to 1859 and a general in the Austrian army. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a> he led Croatian forces against the Hungarian revolutionary government and helped crush the uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">Serfdom</a> in Croatia was abolished early in his time as ban. His equestrian statue stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>'s main square, which bears his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josip-jelacic.jpg" alt="Josip Jelačić" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ivan Zasche (1826-1863) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ban-josip-jelacic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Josip Jelačić was born on 16 October 1801 in Petrovaradin, then part of the Slavonian Military Frontier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy. His father, Baron Franjo Jelačić Bužimski, was a lieutenant field marshal, and his mother was Baroness Anna Portner von Höflein. He was educated at the Theresian Military Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and spoke all the South Slavic languages as well as German, Italian, French and Hungarian. He joined the Austrian army as a lieutenant on 11 March 1819. He led a campaign against Bosnian Ottoman troops at Velika Kladuša on 17 October 1835 and was promoted colonel in 1841.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 22 March 1848 Jelačić was promoted major-general and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Croatian Sabor</a> elected him Ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. He supported Croatian autonomy from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> and took the oath as ban on 5 June 1848. Croatia's demands included union of its provinces, separation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>. On 19 April he proclaimed the union and separation while declaring loyalty to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy. The imperial court first declared him a rebel, then came to see Croatian forces as an ally against the Hungarian Batthyány government.</p><p>On 11 September 1848 he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drava">Drava</a> and marched on Pest. His army was checked at Pákozd on 29 September, and he turned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, where he joined imperial troops and helped suppress the October revolution. On 30 October at Schwechat he defeated the Hungarian army. In the winter campaign he took part in the occupation of Pest-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> on 5 January 1849, but the army of Windisch-Grätz and Jelačić was defeated at Isaszeg on 6 April. He then fought in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, and the last battle there was Hegyes on 14 July. After the revolution he implemented the new constitution of 4 March 1849 and worked under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-bach">Alexander von Bach</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jelačić died on 20 May 1859 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> after an illness and was buried in Zaprešić, near his castle. He was fairly unpopular among the Croatian political elite of his time, including Ante Starčević, but is now considered an important figure of Croatian history. His reputation differs by country: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> he is a national hero, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> he was seen as a rebel, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> he is a very unpopular figure.</p><p>The central square of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> was named Ban Jelačić Square in 1848. A statue by Anton Dominik Fernkorn was erected there in 1866, removed under Communist rule in 1947 and reinstalled in 1990. The patriotic song Ustani bane was written to glorify him, and his portrait appeared on the Croatian 20 kuna banknote.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Josip Jelačić” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1848 Ban <b>Josip Jelačić</b> led Croatian forces against the Hungarian revolution.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-habsburg">Kingdom of Croatia (Habsburg)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Petrovaradin Fortress, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.25,19.86&amp;year=1838" aria-label="Petrovaradin Fortress on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Josip Jelačić"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4393 6201h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4393 6201h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.25,19.86&amp;year=1838">What was here in 1838? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
